;; spec:   §7 (types §2.10, §8.6)
;; expect: accept
;; result: 12
;; audit:  clean
;; swap! must replace the atom's value atomically with (f old), running f
;; again when the atom changed while f ran:
;;   ownership.md §7: "Values that must change together live in one atom
;;   ... and change with one `swap!`, which is already all-or-nothing".
;;   syntax §3.11: "(swap! a f) ... replace atomically with (f old); f may
;;   run more than once".
;;   types §8.6: "(swap! a f): loop { old = @a (retained); new = f(old);
;;   lock; if load == old { ... store new ... } else { unlock; ...;
;;   retry } }".
;; Here f, on its first run only, has another thread add 10 to the atom
;; and waits for it. By §8.6 the compare fails (11 /= 1), f runs again
;; on 11 (the flag is set, so no second update) and stores 12.
;;
;; Observed (fibref run):
;;   result: 2
;;   audit:  clean=true leak-cycles=0 leaks=0 errors=0
;; The +10 made by the other thread is silently lost: the interpreter's
;; swap! stores f's result without comparing (crates/fibref/src/eval/
;; cells.rs: "swap!'s compare always succeeds", justified by the
;; deterministic executor never running two threads at once, but f
;; itself can run another thread to completion, or call swap! on the
;; same atom directly). The same loss happens without a thread:
;;   (swap! a (fn (x) (do (if @once () (do (set! once true)
;;      (swap! a (fn (y) (+ y 10))) ())) (+ x 1))))
;; with once a (cell false) also gives 2 instead of 12.
;; Diagnosis: eval (swap! skips the compare-and-retry of types §8.6), so
;; fibref and a compiled program disagree (method.md rule 6). Note also
;; that a reentrant swap! whose inner update happens on every run of f
;; never terminates under §8.6 (livelock), while fibref returns; the
;; spec does not say what such a program means.
(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((a (atom 1))
        (once (atom false)))
    (swap! a (fn (x)
               (do (if @once
                       ()
                       (do (reset! once true)
                           (join (spawn (fn () (swap! a (fn (y) (+ y 10))))))
                           ()))
                   (+ x 1))))))
